1
00:00:00,279 --> 00:00:03,698
part 1 chapter 7.

2
00:00:03,698 --> 00:00:07,719
If there is hope, wrote Winston, it lies in the pros

3
00:00:07,719 --> 00:00:11,738
If there was hope, it must lie in the polls because only there, in those

4
00:00:11,738 --> 00:00:16,719
swarming disregarded masses, 85 percent of the population of Oceania, could the

5
00:00:16,719 --> 00:00:19,779
force to destroy the Party ever be generated.

6
00:00:19,779 --> 00:00:22,658
The Party could not be overthrown from within.

7
00:00:22,658 --> 00:00:26,560
Its enemies, if it had any enemies, had no way of coming together or even of

8
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:28,198
identifying one another.

9
00:00:28,198 --> 00:00:32,920
Even if the legendary Brotherhood existed, as just possibly it might,

10
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:36,460
it was inconceivable that its members could ever assemble in larger numbers

11
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:38,560
than twos and threes.

12
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,880
Rebellion meant a look in the eyes, an inflection of the voice; at the most, an

13
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:45,099
occasional whispered word.

14
00:00:45,099 --> 00:00:48,279
But the proles, if only they could somehow become conscious of their own

15
00:00:48,279 --> 00:00:51,039
strength, would have no need to conspire.

16
00:00:51,039 --> 00:00:55,960
They needed only to rise up and shake themselves like a horse shaking off flies.

17
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:59,738
If they chose they could blow the Party to pieces tomorrow morning.

18
00:00:59,738 --> 00:01:03,279
Surely sooner or later it must occur to them to do it?

19
00:01:03,279 --> 00:01:08,800
And yet— He remembered how once he had been walking down a crowded street when a

20
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,819
tremendous shout of hundreds of voices— women's voices— —had burst from a side

21
00:01:12,819 --> 00:01:14,679
street a little way ahead.

22
00:01:14,679 --> 00:01:20,979
It was a great formidable cry of anger and despair, a deep loud oh that went

23
00:01:20,979 --> 00:01:23,979
Hamming on like the reverberation of a bell.

24
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:31,300
His heart had leapt it started! he had thought. A riot! The pearls are breaking loose at last!

25
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:35,020
When he had reached the spot it was to see a mob of two or three hundred women

26
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:39,340
crowding around the stalls of a street market, with faces as tragic as though

27
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:42,460
they had been the doomed passengers on a sinking ship.

28
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:47,560
But at this moment the general despair broke down into a multitude of individual corals

29
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:51,340
It appeared that one of the stalls had been selling tin saucepans there were

30
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:56,079
wretched, flimsy things, but cooking pots of any kind were always difficult to get.

31
00:01:56,079 --> 00:02:00,520
Now the supply had unexpectedly given out. The successful women, bumped and

32
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:04,118
jostled by the rest, were trying to make off with their saucepans while dozens of

33
00:02:04,118 --> 00:02:08,380
others clamored round the stall, accusing the stall keeper of favoritism and of

34
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:10,899
having more saucepans somewhere in reserve.

35
00:02:10,899 --> 00:02:15,340
There was a fresh outburst of yells. Two bloated women, one of them with her hair

36
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:18,639
coming down, had got hold of the same saucepan and were trying to tear it out

37
00:02:18,639 --> 00:02:20,260
of one another's hands.

38
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:23,738
For a moment they were both tugging, and then the handle came off.

39
00:02:23,738 --> 00:02:26,260
Winston watched them disgustedly.

40
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:30,340
And yet, just for a moment, what almost frightening power had sounded in that

41
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:34,539
cry from only a few hundred throats! Why was it that they could never shout like

42
00:02:34,539 --> 00:02:36,579
that about anything that mattered?

43
00:02:36,579 --> 00:02:42,460
He wrote: Until they become conscious they will never rebel, and until after they have

44
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:45,520
rebelled they cannot become conscious.

45
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:50,560
That, he reflected, might almost have been a transcription from one of the Party textbooks.

46
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:54,460
The Party claimed, of course, to have liberated the pearls from bondage.

47
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:58,000
Before the Revolution they had been hideously oppressed by the capitalists,

48
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,960
they had been starved and flogged, women had been forced to work in the coal

49
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:05,560
mines (women still did work in the coal mines, as a matter of fact),

50
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,039
children had been sold into the factories at the age of six.

51
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:12,639
But simultaneously, true to the principles of doublethink, the Party

52
00:03:12,639 --> 00:03:16,179
taught us that the pearls were natural inferiors who must be kept in subjection,

53
00:03:16,179 --> 00:03:20,439
like animals, by the application of a few simple rules.

54
00:03:20,439 --> 00:03:25,960
In reality very little was known about the pros It was not necessary to know much.

55
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:31,238
So long as they continued to work and breed, their other activities were without importance.

56
00:03:31,238 --> 00:03:35,560
Left to themselves, like a cattle turned loose upon the plains of Argentina, they

57
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:39,520
had reverted to a style of life that appeared to be natural to them, a sort of

58
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:40,960
ancestral pattern.

59
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:45,158
They were born, they grew up in the gutters, they went to work at 12 they

60
00:03:45,158 --> 00:03:49,420
passed through a brief blossoming period of beauty and sexual desire, they married

61
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:54,460
at 20 they were middle aged at 30 they died, for the most part, at 60.

62
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:58,899
Heavy physical work, the care of home and children, petty quarrels with neighbors

63
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:04,479
films, football, beer and, above all, gambling, filled up the horizon of their minds.

64
00:04:04,479 --> 00:04:08,079
To keep them in control was not difficult. A few agents of the Thought

65
00:04:08,079 --> 00:04:11,859
Police moved always among them, spreading false rumors and marking down and

66
00:04:11,859 --> 00:04:16,300
eliminating the few individuals who were judged capable of becoming dangerous;

67
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:20,738
but no attempt was made to indoctrinate them with the ideology of the Party.

68
00:04:20,738 --> 00:04:24,939
It was not desirable that the pearls should have strong political feelings.

69
00:04:24,939 --> 00:04:28,719
All that was required of them was a primitive patriotism which could be

70
00:04:28,719 --> 00:04:32,319
appealed to whenever it was necessary to make them accept longer working hours or

71
00:04:32,319 --> 00:04:34,118
shorter rations.

72
00:04:34,118 --> 00:04:38,079
And even when they became discontented, as they sometimes did, their discontent

73
00:04:38,079 --> 00:04:42,279
led to nowhere, because, being without general ideas, they could only focus it

74
00:04:42,279 --> 00:04:47,738
on Petty's specific grievances. The larger evils invariably escaped their notice.

75
00:04:47,738 --> 00:04:51,639
The great majority of pearls did not even have telescreens in their homes.

76
00:04:51,639 --> 00:04:54,880
Even civil police interfered with them very little.

77
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:59,020
There was a vast amount of criminality in London, a whole world within a world

78
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:04,420
of thieves, bandits, prostitutes drug Peddlers and racketeers of every description;

79
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:08,439
but since it all happened among the pros themselves, it was of no importance.

80
00:05:08,439 --> 00:05:12,819
In all questions of morals they were allowed to follow their ancestral code.

81
00:05:12,819 --> 00:05:16,599
The sexual puritanism of the Party was not imposed upon them.

82
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:21,340
Promiscuity went unpunished, divorce was permitted. For that matter, even religious

83
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:24,578
worship would have been permitted if the pearls had shown any sign of needing or

84
00:05:24,578 --> 00:05:27,578
wanting it. They were beneath suspicion.

85
00:05:27,578 --> 00:05:32,920
As the Party slogan put it: 'Proles and animals are free.'

86
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:38,679
Winston reached down and cautiously scratched his varicose ulcer. It had begun itching again.

87
00:05:38,679 --> 00:05:42,219
The thing you invariably came back to was the impossibility of knowing what

88
00:05:42,219 --> 00:05:44,920
life before the Revolution had really been like.

89
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:48,760
He took out of the drawer a copy of a children's history textbook which he had

90
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:53,560
borrowed from Mrs Parsons, and began copying a passage into the diary:

91
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:58,779
In the old days [it ran], before the glorious Revolution, London was not the

92
00:05:58,779 --> 00:06:03,460
beautiful city that we know today. It was a dark, dirty, miserable place where

93
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:06,819
hardly anybody had enough to eat and where hundreds and thousands of poor

94
00:06:06,819 --> 00:06:11,139
people had no boots on their feet and not even a roof to sleep under.

95
00:06:11,139 --> 00:06:15,578
Children no older than you had to work 12 hours a day for cruel masters, who

96
00:06:15,578 --> 00:06:19,359
flogged them with whips if they worked too slowly and fed them on nothing but

97
00:06:19,359 --> 00:06:21,760
stale bread crusts and water.

98
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:25,300
But in among all this terrible poverty there were just a few great big

99
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:29,618
beautiful houses that were lived in by rich men who had as many as 30 servants

100
00:06:29,618 --> 00:06:31,420
to look after them.

101
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:35,859
These rich men were called capitalists there were fat, ugly men with wicked

102
00:06:35,859 --> 00:06:38,979
faces, like the one in the picture on the opposite page.

103
00:06:38,979 --> 00:06:42,939
You can see that he is dressed in a long black coat which was called a frock coat,

104
00:06:42,939 --> 00:06:48,219
and a queer, shiny hat shaped like a stovepipe, which was called a top hat.

105
00:06:48,219 --> 00:06:52,779
This was the uniform of the capitalists, and no one else was allowed to wear it.

106
00:06:52,779 --> 00:06:57,279
The capitalists owned everything in the world, and everyone else was their slave.

107
00:06:57,279 --> 00:07:01,779
They owned all the land, all the houses, all the factories, and all the money.

108
00:07:01,779 --> 00:07:05,260
If anyone disobeyed them they could throw them into prison, or they could

109
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:10,300
take his job away and starve him to death whenever any ordinary person spoke to a

110
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:13,719
capitalist he had to cringe and to bow to him, and take off his cap and address

111
00:07:13,719 --> 00:07:15,399
him as 'Sir'.

112
00:07:15,399 --> 00:07:19,840
The chief of all the capitalists was called the King, and—

113
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:23,920
But he knew the rest of the catalog There would be mention of the bishops in

114
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:28,420
their lawn sleeves, the judges in their Urban robes, the pillory, the stocks, the

115
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:32,859
treadmill, the catanine tails, the Lord Mayor's Banquet and the practice of

116
00:07:32,859 --> 00:07:34,779
kissing the Pope's toe.

117
00:07:34,779 --> 00:07:38,920
There was also something called the U.S primary noctis, which would probably not

118
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:41,319
be mentioned in a textbook for children.

119
00:07:41,319 --> 00:07:44,800
It was the law by which every capitalist had the right to sleep with any woman

120
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:47,819
working in one of his factories.

121
00:07:47,819 --> 00:07:50,920
How could you tell how much of it was lies?

122
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:54,520
It might be true that the average human being was better off now than he had

123
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:56,380
been before the Revolution.

124
00:07:56,380 --> 00:08:00,578
The only evidence to the contrary was the mute protest in your own bones, the

125
00:08:00,578 --> 00:08:04,060
instinctive feeling that the conditions you lived in were intolerable and that

126
00:08:04,060 --> 00:08:08,618
at some other time they must have been different struck him that the truly characteristic

127
00:08:08,618 --> 00:08:12,880
thing about modern life was not its cruelty and insecurity, but simply its

128
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:16,060
bareness it's dinginess it's listlessness.

129
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:20,020
Life, if you looked about you, bore no resemblance not only to the lies that

130
00:08:20,020 --> 00:08:25,179
streamed out of the telescreens, but even to the ideals that the Party was trying to achieve.

131
00:08:25,179 --> 00:08:29,920
Great areas of it, even for a Party member, were neutral and non-political, a

132
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:34,478
matter of slogging through dreary jobs, fighting for a place on the Tube, darning

133
00:08:34,478 --> 00:08:39,398
a worn out sock catching a saccharin tablet, saving a cigarette end.

134
00:08:39,398 --> 00:08:44,019
The ideal set up by the Party was something huge, terrible and glittering— —a

135
00:08:44,019 --> 00:08:47,920
world of steel and concrete of monstrous machines and terrifying weapons—

136
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:52,359
—a nation of warriors and fanatics, marching forward in perfect unity, all

137
00:08:52,359 --> 00:08:55,959
thinking the same thoughts and shouting the same slogans, perpetually working,

138
00:08:55,959 --> 00:09:02,138
fighting, triumphing, persecuting 300 million people all with the same face.

139
00:09:02,138 --> 00:09:06,340
The reality was decaying, dingy cities where underfed people shuffled to and

140
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:10,840
fro in leaky shoes, in patched up 19th century houses that smelt always of

141
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:12,939
cabbage and bad lavatories.

142
00:09:12,939 --> 00:09:17,679
He seemed to see a vision of London, vast and ruinous, city of a million dustbins,

143
00:09:17,679 --> 00:09:22,420
and mixed up with it was a picture of Mrs Parsons, a woman with lion face and

144
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:27,058
wispy hair, fiddling helplessly with a blocked waist pipe

145
00:09:27,058 --> 00:09:30,700
He reached down and scratched his ankle again.

146
00:09:30,700 --> 00:09:34,599
Day and nights the telescreens bruised your ears with statistics proving that

147
00:09:34,599 --> 00:09:39,218
people today had more food, more clothes, better houses, better recreations— —that

148
00:09:39,218 --> 00:09:43,780
they lived longer, worked shorter hours, were bigger, healthier, stronger, happier,

149
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:48,760
more intelligent, better educated, than the people of 50 years ago. Not a word of

150
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:51,040
it could ever be proved or disproved.

151
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:55,840
The Party claimed for example, that today 40 of adult pearls were literate: before

152
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:59,859
the Revolution, it was said, the number had only been 15 percent

153
00:09:59,859 --> 00:10:04,058
The Party claimed that the infant mortality rate was now only 160 per

154
00:10:04,058 --> 00:10:08,920
thousand, whereas before the Revolution it had been 300 and so it went on.

155
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:13,299
It was like a single equation with two unknowns. It might very well be that

156
00:10:13,299 --> 00:10:16,540
literally every word in the history books, even the things that one accepted

157
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:19,179
without question, was pure fantasy.

158
00:10:19,179 --> 00:10:22,659
For all he knew there might never have been any such law as the use pre-mind

159
00:10:22,659 --> 00:10:28,659
noctis, or any such creature as a capitalist, or any such garment as a top hat.

160
00:10:28,659 --> 00:10:33,819
Everything faded into mist. The past was erased, the erasure was forgotten, the lie

161
00:10:33,819 --> 00:10:35,379
became truth.

162
00:10:35,379 --> 00:10:39,819
Just once in his life he had possessed— after the event: that was what counted—

163
00:10:39,819 --> 00:10:43,840
—concrete, unmistakable evidence of an act of falsification.

164
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:47,620
He had held it between his fingers for as long as 30 seconds.

165
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:52,120
In 1973, it must have been— —at any rate, it was about at the time when he and

166
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:57,638
Catherine had parted. But the really relevant date was seven or eight years earlier.

167
00:10:57,638 --> 00:11:01,840
The story really began in the middle 60s the period of the great purges in which

168
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:06,579
the original leaders of the Revolution were wiped out once and for all.

169
00:11:06,579 --> 00:11:10,718
By 1970 none of them was left, except Big Brother himself.

170
00:11:10,718 --> 00:11:16,540
All the rest had by that time been exposed as Traders and counter-revolutionaries Goldstein had

171
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:20,260
fled and was hiding no one knew where, and of the others, a few had simply

172
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:24,519
disappeared, while the majority had been executed after spectacular public trials

173
00:11:24,519 --> 00:11:27,879
at which they made confession of their crimes.

174
00:11:27,879 --> 00:11:33,579
Among the last survivors were three men named Jones, Aaronson and Rutherford.

175
00:11:33,579 --> 00:11:37,659
It must have been in 1965 that these three had been arrested.

176
00:11:37,659 --> 00:11:41,739
As often happened, they had vanished for a year or more, so that one did not know

177
00:11:41,739 --> 00:11:45,459
whether they were alive or dead, and then had suddenly been brought forth to

178
00:11:45,459 --> 00:11:48,159
incriminate themselves in the usual way.

179
00:11:48,159 --> 00:11:52,898
They had confessed to intelligence with the enemy (at that date, too, the enemy was

180
00:11:52,898 --> 00:11:57,959
Eurasia), embezzlement of public funds, the murder of various trusted Party members,

181
00:11:57,959 --> 00:12:01,540
intrigues against the leadership of Big Brother which had started long before

182
00:12:01,540 --> 00:12:05,739
the Revolution happened, and acts of sabotage causing the deaths of hundreds

183
00:12:05,739 --> 00:12:07,719
of thousands of people.

184
00:12:07,719 --> 00:12:11,139
After confessing to these things they had been pardoned reinstated in the

185
00:12:11,139 --> 00:12:15,760
Party and given posts which were in fact cynicals but which sounded important.

186
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:20,439
All three had written long, abject articles in the Times analyzing the

187
00:12:20,439 --> 00:12:24,600
reasons for their defection and promising to make amends

188
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:28,478
sometime after their release Winston had actually seen all three of them in the

189
00:12:28,478 --> 00:12:33,398
Chestnut Tree Cafe He remembered the sort of terrified fascination with which he had watched

190
00:12:33,398 --> 00:12:37,359
them out of the corner of his eye there were men far older than himself,

191
00:12:37,359 --> 00:12:41,799
relics of the ancient world, almost the last great figures left over from the

192
00:12:41,799 --> 00:12:45,700
heroic days of the Party. The glamor of the underground struggle in the civil

193
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:50,079
war still faintly clung to them. He had the feeling, though already at that time

194
00:12:50,079 --> 00:12:54,100
facts and dates were growing blurry, that he had known their names earlier than he

195
00:12:54,100 --> 00:12:55,898
had known that of Big Brother.

196
00:12:55,898 --> 00:13:00,818
But also there were outlaws, enemies, untouchables, doomed with absolute

197
00:13:00,818 --> 00:13:03,818
certainty to extinction within a year or two.

198
00:13:03,818 --> 00:13:08,680
No one who had once fallen into the hands of the Thought Police ever escaped in the end

199
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:13,059
there were corpses waiting to be sent back to the grave.

200
00:13:13,059 --> 00:13:16,299
There was no one at any of the tables nearest to them.

201
00:13:16,299 --> 00:13:20,020
It was not wise even to be seen in the neighborhood of such people.

202
00:13:20,020 --> 00:13:23,500
They were sitting in silence before glasses of the gin flavored with cloves

203
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:28,180
which was the specialty of the cafe Of the three, it was Rutherford whose

204
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:30,398
appearance had most impressed Winston.

205
00:13:30,398 --> 00:13:34,719
Rutherford had once been a famous caricaturist, whose brutal cartoons had

206
00:13:34,719 --> 00:13:39,040
helped to inflame popular opinion before and during the Revolution.

207
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:43,299
Even now, at long intervals, his cartoons were appearing in the Times

208
00:13:43,299 --> 00:13:48,459
there was simply an imitation of his earlier manner, and curiously lifeless and unconvincing.

209
00:13:48,459 --> 00:13:53,079
Always they were a rehashing of the ancient themes— —slum tenements, starving

210
00:13:53,079 --> 00:13:58,780
children, street battles, capitalists in top hats— —even on the barricades the capitalists

211
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:04,299
still seemed to cling to their top hats— —an endless, hopeless effort to get back into the past.

212
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:08,978
He was a monstrous man, with a mane of greasy gray hair, his face pouched and

213
00:14:08,978 --> 00:14:11,739
seemed with thick negroid lips.

214
00:14:11,739 --> 00:14:16,239
At one time he must have been immensely strong; now his great body was sagging,

215
00:14:16,239 --> 00:14:19,478
sloping, bulging, falling away in every direction.

216
00:14:19,478 --> 00:14:24,700
He seemed to be breaking up before one's eyes, like a mountain crumbling.

217
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:27,159
It was the lonely hour of fifteen.

218
00:14:27,159 --> 00:14:31,420
Winston could not now remember how he had come to be in the cafe at such a time.

219
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:36,398
The place was almost empty. A tinny music was trickling from the telescreens.

220
00:14:36,398 --> 00:14:40,600
The three men sat in their corner almost motionless, never speaking.

221
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:44,020
Uncommanded, the waiter brought fresh glasses of gin.

222
00:14:44,020 --> 00:14:49,600
There was a chessboard on the table beside them, with the pieces set out but no game started.

223
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:53,920
And then, for perhaps half a minute in all, something happened to the telescreens.

224
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:58,540
The tune that they were playing changed, and the tone of the music changed too

225
00:14:58,540 --> 00:15:03,040
they came into it— —but it was something hard to describe. It was a peculiar,

226
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:08,379
cracked brain jeering note: in his mind Winston called it a yellow note.

227
00:15:08,379 --> 00:15:14,920
And then a voice from the telescreen was singing: Under the spreading chestnut tree I sold

228
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:22,898
you and you sold me: There lie they, and here lie we Under the spreading chestnut tree.

229
00:15:22,898 --> 00:15:27,520
The three men never stirred. But when Winston glanced again at Rutherford's

230
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:31,000
ruinous face, he saw that his eyes were full of tears.

231
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,379
And for the first time he noticed, with a kind of inward shudder, and yet not

232
00:15:35,379 --> 00:15:41,340
knowing at what he shuddered, that both Aaronson and Rutherford had broken noses.

233
00:15:41,340 --> 00:15:46,420
A little later all three were rearrested It appeared that they had engaged in

234
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:49,719
fresh conspiracies from the very moment of their release.

235
00:15:49,719 --> 00:15:54,100
At their second trial they confessed to all their old crimes over again, with a

236
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:55,898
whole string of new ones.

237
00:15:55,898 --> 00:16:02,260
They were executed, and their fate was recorded in the Party histories, a warning to posterity.

238
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:07,059
About five years after this, in 1973, Winston was unrolling a wad of documents

239
00:16:07,059 --> 00:16:10,540
which had just flopped out of the pneumatic tube onto his desk when he

240
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:13,659
came on a fragment of paper which had evidently been slipped in among the

241
00:16:13,659 --> 00:16:18,639
others and then forgotten. The instant he had flattened it out he saw its significance.

242
00:16:18,639 --> 00:16:22,959
It was a half page torn out of the Times of about 10 years earlier—

243
00:16:22,959 --> 00:16:26,379
—the top half of the page, so that it included the date—

244
00:16:26,379 --> 00:16:30,939
—and it contained a photograph of the delegates at some Party function in New York.

245
00:16:30,939 --> 00:16:34,780
Prominent in the middle of the group were Jones, Aaronson and Rutherford.

246
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:40,239
There was no mistaking them; in any case their names were in the caption at the bottom.

247
00:16:40,239 --> 00:16:43,959
The point was that at both trials all three men had confessed that on that

248
00:16:43,959 --> 00:16:46,359
date they had been on Eurasian soil.

249
00:16:46,359 --> 00:16:50,139
They had flown from a secret airfield in Canada to a rendezvous somewhere in

250
00:16:50,139 --> 00:16:54,579
Siberia, and had conferred with members of the Eurasian General Staff, to whom

251
00:16:54,579 --> 00:16:57,459
they had betrayed important military secrets.

252
00:16:57,459 --> 00:17:01,898
The date had stuck in Winston's memory because it chanced to be mid-summer day;

253
00:17:01,898 --> 00:17:06,340
but the whole story must be on record in countless other places as well.

254
00:17:06,340 --> 00:17:12,280
There was only one possible conclusion: the confessions were lies.

255
00:17:12,280 --> 00:17:16,420
Of course, this was not in itself a discovery. Even at that time Winston had

256
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:19,180
not imagined that the people who were wiped out in the purges had actually

257
00:17:19,180 --> 00:17:21,818
committed the crimes that they were accused of.

258
00:17:21,818 --> 00:17:26,439
But this was concrete evidence; it was a fragment of the abolished past, like a

259
00:17:26,439 --> 00:17:31,420
fossil bone which turns up in the wrong stratum and destroys a geological theory.

260
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:34,959
It was enough to blow the Party to atoms, if in some way it could have been

261
00:17:34,959 --> 00:17:39,340
published to the world and its significance made known.

262
00:17:39,340 --> 00:17:41,680
He had gone straight on working.

263
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:45,700
As soon as he saw what the photograph was, and what it meant, he had covered it

264
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:47,559
up with another sheet of paper.

265
00:17:47,559 --> 00:17:53,020
Luckily, when he unrolled it, it had been upside down from the point of view of the telescreen.

266
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:56,799
He took his scribbling pad on his knee and pushed back his chair, so as to get

267
00:17:56,799 --> 00:17:59,318
as far away from the telescreen as possible.

268
00:17:59,318 --> 00:18:03,340
To keep your face expressionless was not difficult, and even your breathing could

269
00:18:03,340 --> 00:18:06,760
be controlled, with an effort: but you could not control the beating of your

270
00:18:06,760 --> 00:18:10,719
heart, and the telescreen was quite delicate enough to pick it up.

271
00:18:10,719 --> 00:18:14,799
He let what he judged to be 10 minutes go by, tormented all the while by the

272
00:18:14,799 --> 00:18:18,760
fear that some accident— —a sudden draft blowing across his desk, for instance—

273
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:20,680
—would betray him.

274
00:18:20,680 --> 00:18:24,520
Then, without uncovering it again, he dropped the photograph into the memory

275
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:27,100
hole, along with some other waste papers.

276
00:18:27,100 --> 00:18:32,398
Within another minute, perhaps, it would have crumbled into ashes.

277
00:18:32,620 --> 00:18:35,260
That was 10 11 years ago.

278
00:18:35,260 --> 00:18:38,439
Today, probably, he would have kept that photograph.

279
00:18:38,439 --> 00:18:42,459
It was curious that the fact of having held it in his fingers seemed to him to

280
00:18:42,459 --> 00:18:46,239
make a difference even now, when the photograph itself, as well as the event

281
00:18:46,239 --> 00:18:48,639
it recorded, was only memory.

282
00:18:48,639 --> 00:18:53,318
Was the Party's hold upon the past less strong, he wondered, because a piece of

283
00:18:53,318 --> 00:18:57,280
evidence which existed no longer had once existed?

284
00:18:57,280 --> 00:19:02,139
But today, supposing that it could be somehow resurrected from its ashes, the

285
00:19:02,139 --> 00:19:04,478
photograph might not even be evidence.

286
00:19:04,478 --> 00:19:08,439
Already, at the time when he made his discovery, Oceania was no longer at war

287
00:19:08,439 --> 00:19:12,520
with Eurasia, and it must have been to the agents of East Asia that the three

288
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:14,978
dead men had betrayed their country.

289
00:19:14,978 --> 00:19:19,959
Since then there had been other changes two, three, he could not remember how many.

290
00:19:19,959 --> 00:19:23,439
Very likely the confessions had been Rewritten and Rewritten until the

291
00:19:23,439 --> 00:19:27,040
original facts and dates no longer had the smallest significance.

292
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:30,879
The past not only changed, but changed continuously.

293
00:19:30,879 --> 00:19:34,420
What most afflicted him with the sense of nightmare was that he had never

294
00:19:34,420 --> 00:19:37,840
clearly understood why the huge imposter was undertaken.

295
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:42,040
The immediate advantages of falsifying the past were obvious, but the ultimate

296
00:19:42,040 --> 00:19:45,939
motive was mysterious. He took up his pain again and wrote:

297
00:19:45,939 --> 00:19:50,379
I understand HOW: I do not understand WHY.

298
00:19:50,379 --> 00:19:54,159
He wondered, as he had many times wondered before whether he himself was a

299
00:19:54,159 --> 00:19:58,120
lunatic. Perhaps a lunatic was simply a minority of one.

300
00:19:58,120 --> 00:20:01,420
At one time it had been a sign of madness to believe that the earth goes

301
00:20:01,420 --> 00:20:05,978
around the sun: today, to believe that the past is unalterable.

302
00:20:05,978 --> 00:20:10,959
He might be alone in holding that belief, and if alone, then a lunatic. But the

303
00:20:10,959 --> 00:20:14,680
thought of being a lunatic did not greatly trouble him: the horror was that

304
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:16,920
he might also be wrong.

305
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:20,439
He picked up the children's history book and looked at the portrait of Big

306
00:20:20,439 --> 00:20:25,659
Brother which formed its frontus piece The hypnotic eyes gazed into his own.

307
00:20:25,659 --> 00:20:29,559
It was as though some huge force were pressing down upon you— —something that

308
00:20:29,559 --> 00:20:33,459
penetrated inside your skull, battering against your brain, frightening you out

309
00:20:33,459 --> 00:20:38,318
of your beliefs, persuading you, almost, to deny the evidence of your senses.

310
00:20:38,318 --> 00:20:42,280
In the end the Party would announce the two and two made five, and you would have

311
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:43,478
to believe it.

312
00:20:43,478 --> 00:20:47,559
It was inevitable that they should make that claim sooner or later: the logic of

313
00:20:47,559 --> 00:20:49,359
their position demanded it.

314
00:20:49,359 --> 00:20:53,379
Not merely the validity of experience, but the very existence of external

315
00:20:53,379 --> 00:20:56,978
reality, was tacitly denied by their philosophy.

316
00:20:56,978 --> 00:20:59,859
The heresy of heresies was common sense.

317
00:20:59,859 --> 00:21:02,739
And what was terrifying was not that they would kill you for thinking

318
00:21:02,739 --> 00:21:05,799
otherwise, but that they might be right.

319
00:21:05,799 --> 00:21:09,159
For, after all, how do we know that two and two make four?

320
00:21:09,159 --> 00:21:13,780
Or that the force of gravity works? Or that the past is unchangeable?

321
00:21:13,780 --> 00:21:17,978
If both the past and the external world exist only in the mind, and if the mind

322
00:21:17,978 --> 00:21:21,000
itself is controllable— —what then?

323
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:25,898
But no! His courage seems suddenly to stiffen of its own accord.

324
00:21:25,898 --> 00:21:31,239
The face of O'Brien, not called up by any obvious association, had floated into his mind.

325
00:21:31,239 --> 00:21:35,620
He knew, with more certainty than before, that O'Brien was on his side.

326
00:21:35,620 --> 00:21:41,020
He was writing the diary for O'Brien— to O'Brien: it was like an interminable

327
00:21:41,020 --> 00:21:44,559
letter which no one would ever read, but which was addressed to a particular

328
00:21:44,559 --> 00:21:47,139
person and took its color from that fact.

329
00:21:47,139 --> 00:21:51,159
The Party told you to reject the evidence of your eyes and ears. It was

330
00:21:51,159 --> 00:21:53,439
their final, most essential command.

331
00:21:53,439 --> 00:21:57,459
His heart sank as he thought of the enormous power arrayed against him, the

332
00:21:57,459 --> 00:22:01,659
ease with which any Party intellectual would overthrow him in debate, the subtle

333
00:22:01,659 --> 00:22:04,959
arguments which he would not be able to understand, much less answer.

334
00:22:04,959 --> 00:22:06,818
And yet he was in the right!

335
00:22:06,818 --> 00:22:09,100
They were wrong and he was right.

336
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:13,059
The obvious, the silly and the true had got to be defended.

337
00:22:13,059 --> 00:22:15,639
Truisms are true, hold on to that!

338
00:22:15,639 --> 00:22:20,680
The solid world exists, its laws do not change. Stones are hard, water is wet,

339
00:22:20,680 --> 00:22:26,020
objects unsupported fall towards the earth's center With the feeling that he was speaking to

340
00:22:26,020 --> 00:22:30,520
O'Brien, and also that he was setting forth an important axiom, he wrote:

341
00:22:30,520 --> 00:22:35,020
Freedom is the freedom to say that two plus two make four. If that is granted,

342
00:22:35,020 --> 00:22:37,000
all else follows
